															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609766879" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609755438 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Jan 2021 10:17</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4861779">
									

<p>The first narrative section feels a lot stronger to me than the second section, in that, it actually has a character and them growing and changing and being affected by this in an emotional way; whereas the second narrative section is really just a bunch of boring tests that convey that hey, this anomaly works exactly as y'know, we were told it works; with a cross-link to a work that's&#8230;not really adding much to either piece.</p>
<p>The problem with the first narrative section is that you can't actually show a lot of the interesting bits in a SCP article: the Foundation isn't really involved in the story, so you kinda have to rely on the person wearing the watch telling us about it, y'know? So my advice here is really 'figure out how you can use the narrative framework of the second section to convey the emotional impact of the first.</p>
<p>I'd also recommend getting rid of the crosstesting: it's generally a bad idea to mix two anomalies when you aren't sure how it'll play out; and so it's generally frowned upon to do it. Beyond this, the actual crosstesting doesn't add much: it's just another random example of this doing the expected thing that's not really that interesting alone.</p>

							</div>

								
					
